# ESP32-S3: OPI PSRAM on the R8 modules, USB-CDC console.
CONFIG_ESP32S3_SPIRAM_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_MODE_OCT=y
CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_80M=y
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_CDC=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Universal MAC address count -- A WIRE-VISIBLE FLEET CONTRACT, NOT A PREFERENCE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This setting decides how the Bluetooth MAC is derived from the factory base MAC, and the
# BLE address IS the device's identity in Home Assistant (its Bluetooth integration keys
# devices on the address). Change it and every deployed unit becomes a NEW device, losing its
# entity history. Nothing errors; the device simply reappears as a stranger.
#
#   FOUR: STA = base+0, AP = base+1, BT = base+2, ETH = base+3   (all universal)
#   TWO:  STA = base+0, BT = base+1                              (universal)
#         AP and ETH become LOCAL, derived by setting the U/L bit 0x02 in octet 0
#
# THE VALUE IS PINNED PER CHIP TO MATCH WHAT THE ARDUINO BUILD SHIPPED, because that is what
# the fleet actually advertises -- not because a rationale was found for it. pioarduino's
# precompiled sdkconfigs set FOUR for esp32/C3/C6 and TWO for the S3, and no rule explains the
# S3 being different: the one distinction that correlates (only the classic ESP32 has an
# internal EMAC) does not, since the C3 has the same profile as the S3 and got FOUR. Most
# likely an inconsistency in arduino-esp32's packaging. Fleet compatibility means matching what
# shipped, correct or not.
#
# Ruled out while investigating (2026-08-03): IDF drift -- `git log -p` on the chip Kconfig.mac
# files shows FOUR as the only default ever set, so arduino's value is not a stale capture; and
# a smaller Espressif allocation for the S3 -- IDF's docs state every chip ships "enough valid
# Espressif universally administered MAC addresses for all internal interfaces", and the
# TWO/FOUR knob is documented as being for CUSTOM MAC ranges. With a factory base MAC either
# value is safe on any chip, which is why this is purely about fleet identity.
#
# PINNED even where it equals IDF's current default: on the S3 this shifted BT from base+1 to
# base+2 purely because the project never named the symbol and inherited a default that
# differed from Arduino's. An unpinned default is a fleet-identity change waiting for an IDF
# upgrade. Do not move these values without a fleet migration plan.
#
# It cannot live in the common sdkconfig.defaults: the symbol is chip-prefixed, and the generic
# CONFIG_ESP_MAC_UNIVERSAL_MAC_ADDRESSES_* is a bare bool with no prompt that this choice
# selects. See docs/DIVERGENCE_MATRIX.md.
# TWO, and this one is a REAL CHANGE from IDF's default of FOUR -- it is the fix for the
# S3 appearing as a new device in Home Assistant after the ESP-IDF port. Confirmed on
# hardware 2026-08-03: base MAC 44:1b:f6:85:b1:b8, the IDF build advertised
# 44:1b:f6:85:b1:ba (base+2) where the Arduino fleet advertises ...b9 (base+1). The
# device NAME was unaffected -- getChipIdHex() reads the BASE mac -- so the name matched
# while the address did not, which is why it read as a new device rather than a broken one.
# The S3 has no internal EMAC (SPI Ethernet chips carry their own MACs), so nothing here
# needs the two addresses FOUR would spend on AP and ETH.
CONFIG_ESP32S3_UNIVERSAL_MAC_ADDRESSES_TWO=y

# CPU FREQUENCY: 240 MHz, the Arduino build's value for this chip. The target had inherited
# IDF's 160 MHz default -- two-thirds the clock, on every path.
#
# S3 ONLY, and that is measured rather than assumed. The Arduino core's own sdkconfigs run
# esp32, esp32c3 and esp32c6 at 160 (160 is the maximum on C3 and C6 anyway); only the S3 is
# 240, which its board definition agrees with (esp32-s3-devkitc-1.json, f_cpu=240000000L).
# So this is the single chip where the port slowed the silicon down.
#
# Every throughput and latency figure in README.md § Verified on hardware was measured at
# 160 MHz with -Og. They are not comparable to the reference firmware's, and they are not
# comparable to this build's either -- re-measure on hardware.
CONFIG_ESP_DEFAULT_CPU_FREQ_MHZ_240=y
